Design Strata, a new standalone product: packet capture and network analysis that AI agents and humans can both use. Michael (2026-10-24): "This tool should have it's own name. This is a new product we are creating. It should be able to get installed onto windows, linux, or ponsos machines."

This task is DESIGN + MOCKUPS ONLY, because his rule is "show me before you start". New local repo <path> (empty). Worktree strata-design, branch MBarc/strata-design. LOCAL ONLY: no push, no GitHub repo.

**Direction already given to Michael. Keep it unless you find a hard reason not to, and say so if you do.**
- **Don't port or rewrite Wireshark.** It is GPLv2: a translation would be a derivative work, and its value lies in roughly 3,000 dissectors.
- **Build our own engine in Rust:**
  - capture through libpcap/AF_PACKET on Linux;
  - agent-oriented analysis that turns traffic into JSON summaries: flows, DNS queries and failures, TCP resets, retransmits and handshake timeouts, TLS SNI, DHCP, ARP conflicts, and Kerberos/LDAP errors (this links to SAM/SDM troubleshooting);
  - optional deep dissection by calling `tshark`, unmodified, when it is installed;
  - standard .pcapng output that opens in Wireshark.
- **Windows capture has a licensing trap:** Npcap's free licence does not allow redistribution with other software without an OEM licence. Evaluate the built-in pktmon/ETW (NDIS capture, no extra driver) against Npcap only when the user already has it installed. Recommend one.

**Everything ships for Windows, Linux (Debian/Ubuntu/Fedora), and PonsOS:**
- one engine, three faces:
  - a CLI (`strata`) over SSH or PowerShell;
  - a GUI (iced, matching the PonsOS/Domain Setup look, which runs on Windows too);
  - a non-interactive JSON mode with fixed exit codes, so Nectra and other agents can drive it.
- Windows wording should feel familiar to admins who know Wireshark, Message Analyzer or pktmon. Linux wording should feel familiar to tcpdump/tshark users.

**Security is the core of the design. Captures contain other people's traffic and sometimes credentials.**
- A small privileged capture service or helper holds CAP_NET_RAW, or the Windows equivalent. Neither the agent nor the GUI ever gets raw-socket rights directly.
- Every capture is bounded by time, size, interface and filter. Files are root/admin-only and auto-deleted after a retention period.
- Redaction: credentials, cookies, Authorization headers and Kerberos/NTLM blobs are stripped from summaries.
- Agents get summaries by default. Raw packets and .pcapng exports need a human admin's approval, the same "a human applies it" rule as the directory and network settings.
- Every capture leaves an audit log entry: who, what, when, and the filter used.
- On PonsOS:
  - it ties into ponsPrivilege and the pons-admins group;
  - it ships an agent knowledge topic (see docs/design/AGENT-KNOWLEDGE.md on PonsOS branch MBarc/ponsos-agent-docs, worktree <path>);
  - the idle RAM pillar is 200 MiB or less, so the helper must cost nothing at idle (socket-activated or started on demand).

**Read for style and integration:**
- <path> the visual spec docs/superpowers/specs/2026-10-20-ponsos-visual-and-remote-design.md, the approved Network app screenshots docs/brand/screens/apps-network.png, and the network settings design on MBarc/ponsos-net-settings (worktree <path>, docs/design/NETWORK-SETTINGS.md).
- The Domain Setup design and mockups: <path>

**Write docs/design/STRATA.md covering:**
- product summary;
- architecture (engine, helper, faces);
- per-OS capture backend and packaging (.deb/.rpm, the PonsOS integration including a Network app tab or its own app, a Windows installer);
- the agent JSON contract, with example outputs for a few troubleshooting stories ("DNS is slow", "can't reach the KDC", "TLS handshake fails");
- security model;
- licensing of every dependency;
- what is deliberately out of scope for v1;
- the test plan;
- open questions for Michael at the top, each with a recommendation.

**Mockups:**
- produce them at 1366x768 and 1920x1080, in docs/mockups/:
  - start a capture (interface, filter, duration, size);
  - live capture;
  - the flows/issues summary;
  - the detail of one finding (e.g. TCP resets to a host);
  - an export pending admin approval;
  - the approval prompt;
  - the read-only view for a non-admin;
  - one screen as it looks on Windows;
- CLI transcripts in docs/cli/:
  - a Linux capture over SSH;
  - a Windows PowerShell capture;
  - an agent JSON run for one troubleshooting story.

Commit. Then send worker_done with the paths and the open questions. A reviewer checks the mockups before Michael sees them.

Create the brand and every repo asset for Strata, the company's new packet-capture and network-analysis product. It runs on Windows, Linux and PonsOS, and serves both AI agents and human admins. Its CLI is `strata`. The name is provisional. It is very lean: a small CLI binary, with the GUI optional.

Michael (2026-10-24): "For strata, come up with it's branding and all the assets needed for the repo".

Repo: <path> Worktree strata-brand, branch MBarc/strata-brand. A parallel worker is writing the product design on MBarc/strata-design (worktree <path>); read its docs/design/STRATA.md if it exists yet. LOCAL ONLY: no push, no GitHub repo.

**Michael's rule is "show me before you start", so work in two phases.**

**PHASE 1 (do now): three distinct brand directions, then stop.**
